<h2>Executive summary</h2>
<div class="card">
  <p><b>(a) Applicability to a billion-scale gallery</b> (≈ {{ gallery_billion }} billion finger records). The three papers are complementary:</p>
  <ul style="margin:2px 0 6px">
    <li><b>AFR-Net</b>: a fixed-length template computed once per print and searchable across the full gallery. Role: <b>candidate retrieval</b>.</li>
    <li><b>JIPNet</b>: accurate pairwise verification with pose estimation, strongest on partial prints. Role: <b>candidate verification</b>.</li>
    <li><b>HERS</b>: search over encrypted templates. Role: <b>template protection</b>.</li>
    <li>Combined in a staged pipeline (a.4), they cover retrieval, verification and privacy.</li>
  </ul>
  <p><b>(b) Partial-print experiment</b> (FVC2002/2004, 160×160 patches):</p>
  <ul style="margin:2px 0">
    <li><b>JIPNet accuracy</b> on the six real-sensor databases: EER {{ real_eer_range }}, TAR at FAR 0.1% of {{ real_tar_range }}, in line with the paper.</li>
    <li><b>JIPNet pose error</b> (typical): {{ rot_range }}° in rotation and {{ shift_range }} px ({{ shift_mm_range }} mm) in translation.</li>
    <li><b>Comparison</b>: JIPNet clearly outperformed the AFR-Net-style fixed-length baseline on the same pairs, as also reported in the JIPNet paper. This confirms the value of pose estimation for partial prints and supports orientation normalisation before template extraction.</li>
    <li><b>Main factors affecting accuracy</b>: fingerprint coverage within the patch, overlap between patches, rotation, and synthetic versus real data.</li>
  </ul>
</div>

<h2>Part (a) · Applicability to billion-scale gallery development</h2>
<p><b>Scale:</b> a billion-scale gallery is taken here as 1 billion people × 10 fingers = {{ gallery_billion }} billion finger records. Every new enrolment is compared against all of them; all derived figures below scale in proportion to the gallery size.</p>
<p><b>Implication:</b> search time and the expected number of false matches both grow in proportion to gallery size. A billion-scale system therefore needs (1) a reusable template for fast full-gallery retrieval, (2) a more accurate verifier applied to a short candidate list, and (3) fusion across fingers.</p>
<p><b>Notation</b> used in the latency and complexity entries: <b>N</b> = number of finger records in the gallery (≈ {{ gallery_billion }} billion); <b>d</b> = numbers per template (template length); <b>K</b> = number of candidates passed to verification; <b>P</b> = local descriptors per print in AFR-Net realignment; <b>n</b> = templates packed into one encrypted block (ciphertext) in HERS; <b>f</b> = fraction of the gallery scanned when an index is used. <b>O(·)</b> describes how cost grows with these quantities, independent of hardware. <b>GFLOP / TFLOP</b> = 10<sup>9</sup> / 10<sup>12</sup> floating-point operations (refer Appendix A0).</p>

<h3>a.1 · AFR-Net: fixed-length fingerprint representation</h3>
<figure class="fig card">{% include "diagrams/afrnet.svg" %}</figure>
<div class="tablewrap card split"><table class="kv">
  <tr><td>Approach</td><td><b>Network</b>: shared ResNet50 trunk with a CNN head and an attention head (85 M parameters, trained on 1.3 M images).<br><b>Template</b>: 384 + 384 numbers per print, compared by cosine similarity.<br><b>Realignment</b>: a pairwise refinement applied only to uncertain scores (0.3–0.6; 17.9% of comparisons in the authors’ tests).</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Reported results</td><td><b>FVC2002 DB1 / DB2 / DB3</b> (full prints, standard FVC protocol, with realignment): TAR at FAR 0.1% of 100% / 99.96% / 99.36%.<br><b>NIST SD14</b> (rolled prints): rank-1 identification 99.78%.<br><b>NIST SD27</b> (latent prints vs a 100,000-print gallery): rank-1 identification 53.10%.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Design choice at scale</td><td>The template is computed once per print, stored, indexed and compatible with encryption, which makes it well suited to <b>candidate retrieval</b> over the full gallery.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Latency</td><td><b>Complexity</b> (machine-independent): template creation O(1) per print, ≈ {{ afr_gflop }} GFLOP (measured operation count); full-gallery search O(N·d) ≈ 2·N·d operations ≈ {{ search_tflop_768 }} TFLOP per enrolee at d = 768 (derived); realignment O(K·P²) with P = 196 local descriptors.<br><b>Creating a template</b> (once per print, at enrolment): 8.42 ms on a GPU, as reported in the paper; {{ afr_template_sec }} s on the CPU test machine (measured).<br><b>Comparing two templates</b>: {{ compare_ns_768 }} ns on one CPU core (measured). Searching all {{ gallery_billion }} billion templates therefore takes ≈ {{ scan_minutes_768 }} minutes on one core (derived), and proportionally less with more cores or an index.<br><b>Realignment</b> (only for uncertain scores): 29.36 ms per pair, as reported, so it is applied to candidate lists only.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Failure cases and impact</td><td><table class="fc">
    <tr><th>Issue</th><th>Impact at billion scale</th><th>Mitigation in the pipeline</th></tr>
    <tr><td><b>Very low image quality</b> (authors)</td><td>Worn, dry or wet fingers produce unreliable templates; a duplicate missed at retrieval is not recovered later.</td><td>Quality check and re-capture at enrolment (stage 0); fusion across 10 fingers.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><b>Very small overlap</b> (authors)</td><td>Partial or off-centre placement reduces the shared area between enrolment and query prints.</td><td>Supervised full-print capture at enrolment; JIPNet verification for partial captures (stage 2).</td></tr>
    <tr><td><b>Rotation on small patches</b> (refer Part (b))</td><td>Template similarity drops as rotation grows (EER {{ afr_eer_db1 }} on rotated 160×160 patches; the JIPNet paper also reports 29–35% for AFR-Net on such patches).</td><td>Orientation normalisation before template extraction (stage 0).</td></tr>
    <tr><td><b>Largest evaluated gallery 100,000</b> (authors)</td><td>False matches grow with gallery size; behaviour at {{ gallery_billion }} billion is not yet demonstrated.</td><td>Validation at million-scale before rollout; multi-finger fusion (stage 3).</td></tr>
  </table></td></tr>
</table></div>

<h3>a.2 · JIPNet: joint pairwise verification and pose estimation</h3>
<figure class="fig card">{% include "diagrams/jipnet.svg" %}</figure>
<div class="tablewrap card split"><table class="kv">
  <tr><td>Approach</td><td><b>Network</b>: shared CNN encoder for both patches, followed by a transformer with self- and cross-attention between them.<br><b>Output</b>: match probability and relative pose (rotation and translation), estimated jointly.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Reported results</td><td><b>Mixed partial-print test set</b> (160×160 patches): EER 4%, TAR at FAR 0.1% of 71% (VeriFinger: 18% and 56%).<br><b>FVC2002 DB1</b> (160×160 patches): EER 1%, TAR at FAR 0.1% of 93%.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Design choice at scale</td><td>A pairwise model with high accuracy on partial prints and an explicit pose output, well suited to <b>candidate verification</b> and to aligning prints for review.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Latency</td><td><b>Complexity</b> (machine-independent): O(K) network runs for K candidates, ≈ {{ jip_gflop }} GFLOP per pair (measured operation count); no reusable template, so a full-gallery search would be O(N) runs ≈ {{ jip_full_text }} FLOP (derived).<br><b>Comparing two patches</b>: {{ jip_sec }} s per pair on the CPU test machine (measured); the paper reports latency on a GPU only as a chart (Fig. 13), without an exact value.<br><b>Verifying a candidate list</b> of 1,000: ≈ {{ jip_1000_minutes }} minutes on the test machine (derived), and considerably less on a GPU.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Failure cases and impact</td><td><table class="fc">
    <tr><th>Issue</th><th>Impact at billion scale</th><th>Mitigation in the pipeline</th></tr>
    <tr><td><b>Severe defects, weak texture</b> (authors)</td><td>Low-quality enrolments may be rejected by verification even when they are true duplicates.</td><td>Fusion with other fingers (and iris/face); manual review queue for borderline cases.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><b>Skin distortion</b> (authors)</td><td>Pressure and skin changes between enrolments years apart; not covered by the single-capture test (refer Part (b)).</td><td>Validation on pairs from different captures before deployment.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><b>Small overlap</b> (authors; refer Part (b))</td><td>Pose error and missed matches increase as overlap decreases.</td><td>Minimum-coverage check at capture; several patches or fingers per decision.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><b>Occasional 180° pose</b> (refer Part (b))</td><td>At most 1.3% of genuine pairs, mostly with high match scores; could support a wrong alignment during review.</td><td>Pose consistency check across fingers (stage 2).</td></tr>
    <tr><td><b>One-to-one only</b> (authors)</td><td>Cannot search the gallery directly: {{ gallery_billion }} billion pairs would take ≈ {{ jip_full_years }} years on the test machine.</td><td>Used only after candidate retrieval (stage 1).</td></tr>
  </table></td></tr>
</table></div>

<h3>a.3 · HERS: search over homomorphically encrypted templates</h3>
<figure class="fig card">{% include "diagrams/hers.svg" %}</figure>
<div class="tablewrap card split"><table class="kv">
  <tr><td>Approach</td><td><b>Template</b>: any fixed-length template, reduced to 64, 32 or 16 numbers.<br><b>Encryption</b>: Fan–Vercauteren homomorphic scheme (Microsoft SEAL); similarity is computed without decrypting the gallery or the query.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Reported results</td><td><b>Search time</b> (100 million gallery, 10 CPU cores): 740 s per query at 32 numbers; ≈ 500 s with a two-stage search.<br><b>Accuracy</b> (NIST SD4 vs 100 million): rank-1 92.3% at 192 and 64 numbers, 92.2% at 32, 78.6% at 16.<br><b>Storage</b>: 140 GB at 32 numbers per 100 million templates. Reported as 275× faster than earlier encrypted search.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Design choice at scale</td><td>Templates remain protected at rest and during search, and any fixed-length template can be used, which makes it the <b>privacy layer</b> for candidate retrieval.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Latency</td><td><b>Complexity</b> (machine-independent): O(d·⌈N/n⌉) encrypted multiplications per query, with n = 4,096 templates packed per ciphertext (paper setting): ≈ {{ hers_mults_text }} at d = 32 and N = {{ gallery_billion }} billion (derived); storage O(N·d).<br><b>Reported</b>: 740 s per query for a 100 million gallery on 10 CPU cores (32 numbers per template).<br><b>Scaled to {{ gallery_billion }} billion</b> (× {{ hers_scale }}): ≈ {{ hers_hours }} hours per query for a full scan (derived). With an index that limits the search to 1% of the gallery: ≈ {{ hers_index_minutes }} minutes (design target).</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Failure cases and impact</td><td><table class="fc">
    <tr><th>Issue</th><th>Impact at billion scale</th><th>Mitigation in the pipeline</th></tr>
    <tr><td><b>Accuracy drop at 16 numbers</b> (authors)</td><td>Limits how far templates can be compressed to save time and storage.</td><td>Use 32–64 numbers per template (≈ {{ storage_enc_tb }} TB encrypted at 32 numbers for {{ gallery_billion }} billion).</td></tr>
    <tr><td><b>Encrypted search cost</b> (authors)</td><td>A full encrypted scan of {{ gallery_billion }} billion is ≈ {{ hers_hours }} hours per query on 10 cores.</td><td>Index that narrows the search (design target 1% of the gallery, ≈ {{ hers_index_minutes }} minutes).</td></tr>
    <tr><td><b>Server cannot rank results</b> (authors)</td><td>Scores must be decrypted by the key holder to select candidates.</td><td>Ranking inside the secure processing zone, with controlled key management.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><b>Synthetic evaluation gallery</b> (authors; refer Part (b))</td><td>Synthetic prints behave differently from real ones (JIPNet EER {{ syn_eer_range }} vs {{ real_eer_range }}), so real-print accuracy at scale is unverified.</td><td>Validation on a large real-print gallery.</td></tr>
  </table></td></tr>
</table></div>

<h3>a.4 · Proposed pipeline</h3>
<figure class="fig card">{% include "diagrams/pipeline.svg" %}</figure>
<div class="tablewrap card"><table>
  <tr><th>Stage</th><th>Supporting evidence</th><th>Time per enrolee</th></tr>
  <tr><td>0 · Capture checks</td><td><b>Coverage:</b> JIPNet EER on FVC2002 DB1 improves from 2.5% to 1.1% when both patches are at least 70% fingerprint.<br><b>Orientation:</b> fixed-length templates perform best on consistently oriented prints.<br><b>Liveness:</b> synthetic prints score highly against each other (refer Part (b)).</td><td>Negligible</td></tr>
  <tr><td>1 · Candidate retrieval</td><td><b>Scalability:</b> fixed-length templates can be searched across the full gallery (a.1).<br><b>Privacy:</b> templates can stay encrypted during search (a.3).<br><b>Compactness:</b> 32–64 numbers per template retain accuracy (HERS).</td><td>Unencrypted: ≈ {{ scan_minutes_768 }} minutes on one core for a full scan, reduced by more cores and indexing. Encrypted: ≈ {{ hers_index_minutes }} minutes on 10 cores if an index limits the search to 1% (derived).</td></tr>
  <tr><td>2 · Candidate verification</td><td><b>Accuracy on partial prints:</b> on the same pairs, JIPNet EER {{ jip_same_range }} versus {{ afr_range }} for a fixed-length template (refer Part (b)).<br><b>Pose output:</b> supports alignment checks and examiner review.</td><td>For 1,000 candidates: ≈ 29 s with AFR-Net realignment; ≈ {{ jip_1000_minutes }} minutes with JIPNet on the CPU test machine.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>3 · Decision</td><td><b>Multi-finger fusion:</b> combining 10 fingers adds independent evidence and reduces false matches at gallery scale.</td><td>Negligible</td></tr>
</table></div>
<div class="tablewrap card"><b class="sans">Machine-independent cost per new enrolee</b>
<table>
  <tr><th>Step</th><th>Complexity</th><th>Operations at N = {{ gallery_billion }} billion</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Template creation (AFR-Net)</td><td>O(1) per print</td><td>≈ {{ afr_gflop }} GFLOP per print, × 10 fingers</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Full-gallery search, plain</td><td>O(N·d)</td><td>≈ {{ search_tflop_768 }} TFLOP at d = 768; ≈ {{ search_tflop_32 }} TFLOP at d = 32</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Indexed search (scans a fraction f)</td><td>O(f·N·d)</td><td>f = 1%: ≈ {{ indexed_gflop_32 }} GFLOP at d = 32</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Full-gallery search, encrypted (HERS)</td><td>O(d·⌈N/n⌉) ciphertext multiplications</td><td>≈ {{ hers_mults_text }} at d = 32, n = 4,096</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Candidate verification (JIPNet)</td><td>O(K)</td><td>K = 1,000: ≈ {{ jip_k1000_tflop }} TFLOP</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Decision (fusion)</td><td>O(K·fingers)</td><td>negligible</td></tr>
</table>
<p class="src">N = gallery size (finger records), d = numbers per template, K = candidate-list length, n = templates per ciphertext. GFLOP/TFLOP = 10<sup>9</sup>/10<sup>12</sup> floating-point operations; network counts measured with PyTorch’s operation counter (AFR-Net: the JIPNet authors’ re-implementation at 224×224 input). These counts are independent of hardware; the wall-clock times above are one example on the test machine. Note that verifying 1,000 candidates with JIPNet requires more operations than a plain scan of the entire gallery at d = 32, which is why the candidate list must remain short.</p>
</div>
<div class="card"><b class="sans">Operating modes</b>
<ul style="margin:4px 0 6px">
  <li><b>Full-print enrolment</b> (supervised capture of full impressions): AFR-Net reports TAR at FAR 0.1% of 99.36–100% on full FVC2002 prints, where its built-in alignment can orient the print. An AFR-Net-type template can therefore handle both retrieval and verification, entirely in encrypted form.</li>
  <li><b>Partial or low-quality captures</b> (small sensors, poor placement): JIPNet verifies the candidate list inside the secure processing zone, where its pose estimation gives the largest benefit (refer Part (b), section b.2).</li>
</ul>
<table>
  <tr><th></th><th>AFR-Net-type template</th><th>JIPNet</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Stored per finger</td><td>768 numbers ≈ 3 KB (float32)</td><td>Fingerprint image (e.g. 388×374 px ≈ 145 KB uncompressed)</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Full gallery, {{ gallery_billion }} B fingers</td><td>≈ {{ storage_plain_tb }} TB unencrypted; ≈ {{ storage_enc_tb }} TB encrypted at 32 numbers (derived)</td><td>Image archive for all fingers</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Matching in encrypted form</td><td>Supported (HERS)</td><td>Requires decrypted images, within the secure zone</td></tr>
</table>
</div>

<h2>Part (b) · JIPNet and AFR-Net-style baseline on FVC2002/2004</h2>
<div class="card"><ul style="margin:2px 0">
  <li><b>Data:</b> FVC2002 and FVC2004, 8 databases (6 real sensors, 2 synthetic), each with 100 fingers × 8 impressions.</li>
  <li><b>Test pairs:</b> 160×160 patches, following the JIPNet protocol (patch centres up to 100 px apart, rotation between −180° and 180°). Per database: 800 genuine pairs (same finger) and 2,400 impostor pairs (different fingers).</li>
  <li><b>Ground-truth pose:</b> both patches of a genuine pair are cut from one impression with a known shift and rotation, so the true pose is exact. Results are therefore an upper bound for pairs taken from different captures.</li>
  <li><b>JIPNet:</b> official code and weights; outputs reproduced to 2 decimals on the authors’ examples. Run on all pairs of all 8 databases.</li>
  <li><b>AFR-Net-style baseline:</b> AFR-Net weights released by the JIPNet authors, used as a fixed-length template (score = 0.2 × CNN similarity + 0.8 × attention similarity). Run on a subsample: 400 genuine pairs each from FVC2002 DB1 and DB3, against all different-finger combinations.</li>
  <li><b>Metrics:</b> <i>EER</i>, the error rate at the threshold where false accepts equal false rejects (lower is better). <i>TAR at FAR 0.1%</i>, the share of genuine pairs accepted when only 1 in 1,000 impostor pairs is accepted (higher is better). <i>Pose error</i>, the difference between estimated and true rotation (degrees) and translation (pixels).</li>
  <li><b>Test machine:</b> 2-core Intel Xeon CPU (2.1 GHz), no GPU. Measured timings refer to this machine; a GPU server is considerably faster.</li>
</ul></div>

