<h3>b.4 · Failure analysis</h3>
<div class="tablewrap card"><table>
  <tr><th>Factor</th><th>Observation and mitigation</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Fingerprint coverage</td><td>JIPNet EER on FVC2002 DB1 improves from 2.5% to 1.1% (paper: 1%) when both patches are ≥ 70% fingerprint (Figure A5). Lowest-scoring genuine pairs often contain blank regions (Figure A6).</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Overlap</td><td>Rotation and translation errors increase as the overlap between patches decreases (Figure A4).</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Synthetic prints</td><td>EER {{ syn_eer_range }} versus {{ real_eer_range }} on real sensors; different synthetic fingers receive high scores (Figure A7). This suggests synthetic galleries are not representative of real performance, and that resistance to synthetic prints (e.g. “MasterPrint” attacks, Roy et al. 2017; Bontrager et al. 2018) should be part of evaluation.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>180° pose estimates</td><td>Rotation error above 150° in at most 1.3% of genuine pairs (0–0.3% on real sensors), mostly with high match scores; a pose consistency check addresses this.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Rotation (fixed-length)</td><td>Accuracy of the AFR-Net-style template decreases with rotation (refer Part (b), section b.2); orientation normalisation before template extraction addresses this.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Sensor type</td><td>Consistent performance on real sensors not seen in training (optical FVC2002 DB2, thermal FVC2004 DB3).</td></tr>
</table></div>

<h2 class="pb">Appendix</h2>
<div class="card"><b class="sans">A0 · Note on operation counts (GFLOP)</b>
<p>A <b>FLOP</b> is one floating-point operation: a single addition or multiplication of two decimal numbers. A <b>GFLOP</b> is one billion such operations. For example, creating one AFR-Net template takes ≈ {{ afr_gflop }} GFLOP, and comparing one JIPNet pair takes ≈ {{ jip_gflop }} GFLOP.</p>
<p>The operation count is fixed by the method and does not depend on the hardware, which is why it is used here as a machine-independent measure of cost. Hardware speed is expressed separately in <b>GFLOPS</b> (operations per second). The time for a step is approximately the operation count divided by the hardware speed, so the same count gives shorter times on faster hardware such as a GPU.</p>
</div>
<h3>Supporting figures</h3>
